          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix the {{fish-sauce}}, lime juice, and {{palm-sugar}} in a bowl. Stir until the sugar dissolves. Taste. The dressing should be sour first, then salty."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Bring a large saucepan of water to the boil. Add the fish chunks and poach for 2 to 3 minutes until just opaque. Remove with a slotted spoon."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the prawns to the same water. Cook for 2 minutes until just pink. Remove and drain."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "While still warm, combine the seafood with the shallots, spring onions, lemongrass, and chilli in a large bowl."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour the dressing over and toss gently."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Top with fresh coriander and serve immediately with jasmine rice."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Seafood is rubbery",
              "cause": "Overcooked",
              "fix": "Pull the seafood from the water the moment the colour changes. It carries over cooking in the warm dressing."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Salad is watery and the dressing has diluted",
              "cause": "Seafood not well drained before dressing",
              "fix": "Shake off excess water and pat gently before adding to the bowl."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Lemongrass is tough to eat",
              "cause": "Outer layers not removed or sliced too thickly",
              "fix": "Remove the tough outer layers and slice the inner stalk as thinly as possible."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Yum talay is the Thai seafood salad. It appears on menus at seafood restaurants along the Gulf and Andaman coasts and in Bangkok. The word yum means tossed or mixed salad in Thai. The dressing is the same four-balance formula used in larb and som tum. What varies is the protein and the aromatics. Lemongrass sliced directly into the salad is a northern and central Thai touch that adds texture and fragrance beyond the dressing alone. This dish is always eaten fresh."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
